{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"_data": [], "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "id": 0, "template_override": 1}, {"contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe8n2le02ob10re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<p><strong>Rick</strong>: Hey, Darryl! Do we need to use UML or UML 2.0 for this task?<br /><strong>Darryl</strong>: Well, I think it's better to use UML 2.0 because it has a different dimension in the group of Unified Modeling Language. I mean, it is more complex and that can help us.<br /><strong>Rick</strong>: I thought they were identical.<br /><strong>Darryl</strong>: No, they are not, in 1997 they created UML as a standard language to construct, visualize and document the artifacts of software systems.</p>"}, {"time": "31000", "image": "enpe8n2le02ob10re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<p><strong>Rick</strong>: But wait&hellip; Do we need to understand UML as a programming language?<br /><strong>Darryl</strong>: No, it is not a programming language, but we can use its tools to produce codes in different languages using UML diagrams. With UML 2.0 we have the advantage of adding new features that we can use in an extensive way to develop models and their corresponding systems; that&rsquo;s why I think it can be useful.</p>"}, {"time": "60000", "image": "enpe8n2le02ob10re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<p><strong>Rick: </strong>Oh, ok. Do we have a way to differentiate UML from UML 2.0?</p>\n<p><strong>Darryl</strong>: Actually, there are just two parts that describe the new dimensions in UML 2.0. Firstly, its infrastructure. It defines the basic constructs of the language and you use it to give mechanisms of adjustment, providing a reusable meta- language core. Secondly, its superstructure; it defines the users' constructs and you use to provide better support and better options for the software modeling and behavior, improving the constructs.<br /><strong>Rick</strong>: I suppose that they created the new structure to answer a goal of restructuring and polishing UML, and to make its usage, implementation, and adaptation simpler.<br /><strong>Darryl</strong>: That's correct! And that is why I told you to use 2.0.</p>"}], "audiosrc": "enpe8n2le02ob10re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe8n2le02ob10re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "title": "", "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "_data": []}], "exercises": [{"questions": [], "stimuli_ids": [0], "type": "WLC", "id": 0, "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "I can talk about the basic concept for UML and differences with UML 2.0.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can talk about the new dimensions that appear in UML 2.0.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can discuss the uses of UML infrastructure and UML superstructure.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "What do you use UML for?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "How many versions of UML do you know?", "type": "Free Header Item"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"correct_feedback": "Correct", "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "allowed_attempts": 2}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": "Ask your partner:", "type": "free_header_text"}, {"content": "Unified Modeling Language's New Version", "type": "free_type_text_area"}], "template_override": 1, "img_path": ""}, {"questions": [{"responses": [{"selected": 0, "_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "id": 0, "correct": 0}, {"selected": 0, "_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "id": 1, "correct": 1}], "id": 0, "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "type": "question"}]}], "stimuli_ids": [1], "type": "STI", "id": 1, "template_override": 1, "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": " Darryl explained Rick the differ